From: Cloudy Pad <dev@cloudypad.gg>
Subject: [PATCH] headless: report hardware cursor as unsupported

The headless backend's set_cursor stub reports hardware cursor support
(return true) while having no way to display a cursor: there is no cursor
plane on headless outputs. wlroots then marks the cursor as the hardware
cursor and skips it in software rendering, so the cursor is never
composited into the output buffer. Screen capture clients (Sunshine via
wlr-screencopy or wlr-export-dmabuf) copy the output buffer and the
cursor is invisible in the stream, while input events keep working.

Returning false keeps the cursor on the software path, where the scene
renderer composites it into the output buffer: the cursor becomes visible
to capture clients, matching what users see on real hardware (hardware
cursor planes) and other headless setups.
---
 backend/headless/output.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/backend/headless/output.c b/backend/headless/output.c
index a4cdb17..2cf10af 100644
--- a/backend/headless/output.c
+++ b/backend/headless/output.c
@@ -81,7 +81,11 @@ static bool output_commit(struct wlr_output *wlr_output,

 static bool output_set_cursor(struct wlr_output *wlr_output,
 		struct wlr_buffer *buffer, int hotspot_x, int hotspot_y) {
-	return true;
+	// Report failure: the headless backend cannot actually display a
+	// hardware cursor (no cursor plane). Returning false keeps the cursor
+	// on the software path, where it is composited into the output buffer
+	// and thus visible to wlr-screencopy clients (eg. Sunshine).
+	return false;
 }

 static bool output_move_cursor(struct wlr_output *wlr_output, int x, int y) {
	return true;
 }
